    "story_text": "Hi HN,<p>I’ve been working on esp32sim, an open-source ESP32 emulator that runs in the browser.<p>The fun part of the demo is that there is no ESP32 involved — the firmware is executing in the emulator.<p>The goal is to make ESP32 software easier to experiment with, test and simulate without always needing physical hardware connected.<p>Source:\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;mikroverk&#x2F;esp32sim\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;mikroverk&#x2F;esp32sim</a><p>I made a short video showing it in action:\n<a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;waoWFilTWEc\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;youtu.be&#x2F;waoWFilTWEc</a><p>I’m particularly interested in feedback from embedded developers: what would an emulator like this need to support before you’d actually use it for development, testing or CI?",
